{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Gwk3kGdWuihvZiK9YEo2Sc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JAD7rMhkuRPsV4hptFLkGz","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_4/projects/trace-verdict-study/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T113804/best/paper.tex","limit":50},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4944,"cache_read_input_tokens":77211,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4944,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01VHy7McLC726H2YkX7Buc3b","session_id":"dcb29bac-0892-40d8-9a83-9cc5c54535a5","uuid":"aedd2933-9063-4d37-af81-31d4f6a4cfe3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JAD7rMhkuRPsV4hptFLkGz","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n11\t\\usepackage[colorlinks=true,citecolor=blue,urlcolor=blue]{hyperref}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{Exception Events Are All You Need: Diagnostic Signal Locality \\\\in Execution Trace Verdict Prediction}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{Anonymous}\n16\t\n17\t\\begin{document}\n18\t\\maketitle\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{abstract}\n21\tExecution traces are increasingly used to improve code Large Language Model (LLM) performance, yet no prior work has identified which trace components drive verdict prediction (binary pass/fail classification). We reframe verdict prediction as fault detection and conduct a systematic ablation across four code LLMs spanning three model families and two parameter scales (1.5B to 13B). We find that exception events, compact structured error summaries occupying roughly 400--500 tokens at the trace tail, consistently achieve the highest accuracy across all models (95--99\\%), outperforming full traces (81.8\\%) by up to 18 percentage points. A causal decomposition of the full-trace-to-compressed-trace gap reveals that information overload accounts for 74\\% of the deficit while gradient dilution contributes only 26\\%. Component ablation uncovers a strict two-tier diagnostic hierarchy: Tier~1 signals (exceptions, assertion outcomes) are individually near-perfect and sufficient; Tier~2 signals (loop summaries) carry moderate information but are fully subsumed. A token budget analysis across all four models reveals a universal sharp phase transition between 384 and 512 tail tokens (mean gradient ratio 8.8:1), confirming that diagnostic information is highly localized in the final trace segment. Cross-seed validation (3 seeds per model) shows tight convergence bands (max standard deviation 0.9 percentage points), and LoRA training stability emerges as the practical bottleneck differentiating model families. These findings establish that verdict-optimized trace design should extract the highest-specificity diagnostic signal and discard everything else.\n22\t\\end{abstract}\n23\t\n24\t\\section{Introduction}\n25\t\n26\tExecution traces provide a detailed record of program runtime behavior, including variable states, control flow paths, and exception events. Recent work has explored using traces to improve code LLM capabilities through supervised fine-tuning (SFT). DCSH \\cite{ni2024dcsh} evaluated five trace formats across generative code tasks and concluded that ``no single trace representation consistently outperforms others.'' TracePile \\cite{tracepile2025} scaled trace-augmented pretraining to 2.6 million samples but focused exclusively on code generation, not classification. TRACED \\cite{traced2024} demonstrated that traces help encoder-based test verdict classification but did not investigate which trace components are responsible. A fundamental question remains unanswered: what minimal diagnostic signal in an execution trace is sufficient for accurate verdict prediction?\n27\t\n28\tThis question matters for two practical reasons. First, full execution traces are prohibitively long (often thousands of tokens), causing both computational overhead and, as we show, information overload that degrades model performance. Second, deployment at scale requires trace representations that are robust across model families, parameter counts, and training seeds, not just effective on a single architecture.\n29\t\n30\tExisting trace formats are designed for execution simulation: they preserve sequential state changes so that a model can mentally re-execute the program. For verdict prediction, however, the task reduces to fault detection, and the relevant information may be far more localized. We hypothesize that the diagnostic moment, specifically the exception event indicating a runtime fault, concentrates the vast majority of verdict-relevant signal, and that the surrounding trace context is largely redundant.\n31\t\n32\tWe test this hypothesis through a systematic study across four code LLMs from three families (Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B, Qwen-2.5-Coder-1.5B, DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B, CodeLlama-13B-Instruct). Our contributions are:\n33\t\n34\t\\begin{enumerate}\n35\t\\item \\textbf{Exception dominance is universal.} Exception-only SFT achieves the highest verdict accuracy across all four models and three model families (99.8\\% on Qwen-7B, 97.2\\% mean on CodeLlama-13B with 3-seed validation), consistently outperforming full traces and compressed traces (Section~\\ref{sec:exception_dominance}).\n36\t\\item \\textbf{Causal decomposition of the trace compression gap.} A label-only loss ablation quantifies that information overload (74\\%) dominates gradient dilution (26\\%) in explaining why full traces underperform compressed representations (Section~\\ref{sec:causal}).\n37\t\\item \\textbf{Two-tier diagnostic hierarchy.} Component ablation reveals a strict hierarchy: Tier~1 signals (exceptions, assertion outcomes; each individually $\\geq$99.4\\%) subsume Tier~2 signals (loop summaries; 85.6\\%), and combining tiers introduces noise rather than benefit (Section~\\ref{sec:hierarchy}).\n38\t\\item \\textbf{Universal sharp phase transition in token budget curves.} Across all four models, verdict accuracy undergoes a sharp transition between 384 and 512 tail tokens (mean $\\Delta_{384 \\to 512}$ = 17.1 percentage points, gradient ratio 8.8:1), confirming that diagnostic information is concentrated in the final $\\sim$400--500 tokens (Section~\\ref{sec:phase_transition}).\n39\t\\item \\textbf{LoRA training stability as practical bottleneck.} Cross-seed experiments (3 seeds $\\times$ 4 models) reveal tight post-transition convergence bands (max std = 0.9pp) but expose LoRA initialization sensitivity as the key factor differentiating model families (Section~\\ref{sec:training_stability}).\n40\t\\end{enumerate}\n41\t\n42\t\\section{Related Work}\n43\t\n44\t\\paragraph{Trace-augmented code LLMs.}\n45\tSeveral recent efforts incorporate execution traces into code LLM training. DCSH \\cite{ni2024dcsh} compared five trace representations (step-by-step, compressed, output-only, assertion-level, and variable-focused) on generative code tasks, finding inconsistent results across formats and models. TracePile \\cite{tracepile2025} assembled 2.6M trace-augmented samples for continued pretraining and observed modest downstream gains on code generation benchmarks. Both studies focus on generative tasks and do not evaluate verdict prediction. TRACED \\cite{traced2024} is closest to our setting: it trains encoder-based classifiers on execution traces for test verdict prediction and demonstrates that traces improve over code-only baselines. However, TRACED treats traces as monolithic inputs without decomposing which components carry diagnostic signal.\n46\t\n47\t\\paragraph{Verdict prediction and fault localization.}\n48\tTest verdict prediction (classifying test executions as pass or fail) is a well-studied software engineering task, traditionally addressed with hand-crafted features from coverage matrices or statistical fault localization techniques \\cite{wong2016survey}. LLM-based approaches are more recent: CodeT \\cite{chen2023codet} uses dual execution to filter generated solutions, and similar execute-then-classify pipelines appear in competitive programming evaluators. These approaches treat the execution oracle as a black box. Our work opens this box and asks what trace information the classifier actually needs.\n49\t\n50\t\\paragraph{Execution reasoning in LLMs.}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01VHy7McLC726H2YkX7Buc3b","session_id":"dcb29bac-0892-40d8-9a83-9cc5c54535a5","uuid":"cf5aad22-a227-4907-96c7-98a9f3ee15d9","timestamp":"2026-05-18T12:25:18.291Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a6c99a2559f41878a","tool_use_id":"toolu_01X88DADNBF2BNK2aeab7Kos","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":93393,"tool_uses":18,"duration_ms":142742},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"b77bfd7c-bcc2-4ce1-94c2-83a6f9ef0ed7","session_id":"dcb29bac-0892-40d8-9a83-9cc5c54535a5"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SoCVjrYB2kawknZFNhSdmE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NX6KCyqSyuZhbfyH6gNXqY","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"trace-verdict-study","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260518T113804","overall_score":"Weak Accept (4)","report":"\n# Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper studies which components of execution traces are sufficient for binary pass/fail verdict prediction. Through systematic ablation across four code LLMs (1.5B--13B, three families), the authors find that exception events alone achieve 99.33--99.83% accuracy, dramatically outperforming full traces (81.76%). The paper decomposes the full-trace deficit into information overload (~74%) and gradient dilution (~26%), identifies a two-tier diagnostic hierarchy among trace components, and reports a sharp phase transition at ~400--500 tail tokens. The experimental coverage is extensive and the core finding is convincing, though several methodological concerns limit the strength of the claimed contributions.\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **Comprehensive ablation design.** The paper systematically varies trace components (exception, assertion, loop, combinations, no-trace), truncation direction (head vs. tail), token budgets (256--2048), loss masking, and format markers. This is one of the most thorough ablation studies I have seen for trace-based code tasks. The experimental matrix is well-structured and the conditions are clearly motivated.\n\n- **Multi-model validation.** Testing across four models from three architecture families (Qwen, DeepSeek, CodeLlama) at two scales (1.5B, 6.7B/7B, 13B) substantially strengthens the generalizability claim. The finding that exception-only accuracy is nearly identical (0.56pp range excluding the DeepSeek outlier) across these diverse architectures is compelling.\n\n- **Strong baselines and controls.** The inclusion of regex baselines (6 variants), zero-shot baselines (4 models), few-shot baselines, a format-marker ablation (nomarker), and a label-only loss ablation demonstrates methodological rigor. The regex baselines in particular are valuable for understanding what fraction of the signal is trivially extractable.\n\n- **Transparent reporting of failures.** The paper openly discusses the DeepSeek seed-123 outlier (91.09%), the CodeLlama tail-2048 training failures requiring retraining, the tokenizer-driven truncation confound for DeepSeek VOTA, and the format-marker shortcut (B5 at 99.33%). This honesty strengthens credibility.\n\n- **Statistical methodology.** Paired bootstrap resampling, McNemar's tests, Wilson CIs, ECE/Brier calibration metrics, and cross-seed validation are all appropriate and well-applied. The explicit reporting of p-values for key comparisons is good practice.\n\n- **Irreducible error analysis.** The root-cause analysis of the 2 persistent errors (GeneratorExit false positives from HumanEval/95) is thorough and actionable. The observation that CodeLlama seed 123 achieves 100% demonstrates these are convergence artifacts, not hard limits.\n\n- **DebugBench cross-dataset evaluation.** Testing on an independent dataset with a different fault distribution (synthetic mutations vs. LLM-generated errors) adds generalization evidence beyond the primary benchmark.\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **The core finding is arguably unsurprising, and the paper overstates its novelty.** For binary verdict prediction, the presence/absence of an unhandled exception is nearly definitional of pass/fail for the failure modes in these benchmarks (TypeError, ValueError, etc.). The B5 regex baseline achieving 99.33% by detecting a format marker, and B4 achieving 93.61% by matching exception class names, confirms that this is largely a pattern-matching task. The 0.50pp SFT advantage over B5 (p=0.041) is statistically significant but practically marginal. Framing this as five distinct contributions inflates the perceived novelty.\n\n- **Dataset is too small and homogeneous to support the universality claims.** 591 problems from HumanEval + MBPP, with solutions generated by a single model (Qwen-7B-Instruct), is a narrow evaluation setting. The 71.5%/28.5% class imbalance means only ~339 fail samples in the test set. The paper's failure distribution is dominated by standard Python exceptions (TypeError, ValueError, IndexError), which are the easiest failure modes to detect. Silent logic errors (where the program produces wrong output without raising an exception) are acknowledged in the Limitations but represent a critical gap: for such errors, the exception-only approach would be blind. The paper title (\"Exception Events Are All You Need\") overpromises given this limitation.\n\n- **The \"causal decomposition\" claim is not truly causal.** The label-only loss ablation changes two things simultaneously: (1) it eliminates gradient dilution on trace tokens, but (2) it also changes the effective learning rate on the verdict token (since the loss is now concentrated on a single token rather than distributed). The authors acknowledge the decomposition is \"approximate\" and that \"the two factors may interact,\" but still present 26%/74% as a precise causal split. This is a correlational decomposition at best. A truly causal analysis would require controlled interventions that isolate each mechanism independently.\n\n- **Phase transition claim is mechanistically trivial.** The \"sharp phase transition at 384--512 tokens\" is explained by the fact that exception tracebacks are 150--400 tokens long and appear at the trace end. When the tail window is too small to capture the traceback, accuracy is low; when it captures it, accuracy is high. This is not a phase transition in any meaningful physical or information-theoretic sense; it is a truncation artifact. The gradient ratio metric (8.8:1) quantifies the steepness of a step function caused by including vs. excluding the diagnostic signal, which is expected by construction. The universality across models simply confirms that all models can use exception information when it is present and cannot when it is absent.\n\n- **OOD cross-language evaluation (Java/JavaScript) appears to be imagined, not real.** The dream report's review_context.yaml explicitly lists this as an \"imagined experiment\" under plan_021 with approximate (~) accuracy values. The actual paper (docs/paper/) does not contain a cross-language OOD section. If these results are included in the dream-report version of the paper, they should be flagged as fabricated. The actual paper uses DebugBench as its cross-dataset evaluation, which is more credible but remains Python-only.\n\n- **Frontier model comparison (GPT-4o, Claude) is also imagined.** The review_context.yaml shows these as plan_020 imagined experiments with predicted values (GPT-4o 3-shot ~95.1%, Claude 3-shot ~96.1%). The actual paper does not include these comparisons; instead it has real zero-shot and 3-shot results for the four open-source models. The dream paper version includes these numbers with ~ prefixes, which is a red flag. If presented as real results, this is a serious integrity concern.\n\n- **Component ablation is single-model for key conditions.** ae_only and loop_only are evaluated only on Qwen-7B. The paper justifies this as \"targeting the trace composition mechanism rather than cross-model generalization,\" but the two-tier hierarchy is presented as a general finding. Without cross-model validation of loop_only and ae_only, the hierarchy could be Qwen-specific.\n\n- **Test set duplication inflates effective sample size.** The exception_only test set has 48.99% duplicate inputs (607 unique of 1,190). The paper treats all 1,190 as independent Bernoulli trials for CI computation. Under deduplication, the effective test set shrinks substantially and CIs widen (the paper acknowledges Qwen accuracy drops to 99.67% with CI [98.81, 99.91] under n=607). This is not adequately addressed in the main text. The near-identical passing traces (no exception = same input regardless of solution) mean the model is being tested on the same input multiple times.\n\n- **Self-familiarity bias.** Solutions are generated by Qwen-7B-Instruct, which is also one of the evaluation models. While the paper notes this \"does not affect verdict prediction,\" it may affect the pass/fail distribution and error characteristics in ways that favor models from the same family. The Limitations section acknowledges this but it deserves more attention.\n\n- **Missing important baselines.** (1) No comparison with encoder-based models (e.g., CodeBERT, TRACED's approach) on the same task. TRACED is cited as closest prior work but never compared against directly. (2) No comparison with simple MLP/logistic regression on exception features, which would clarify how much of the SFT benefit comes from the LLM architecture vs. the feature itself.\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. What fraction of fail-class samples in your dataset involve silent logic errors (wrong output, no exception raised)? If exception_only achieves 99.83% overall, and the only errors are 2 GeneratorExit FPs, does this mean every fail-class sample with a genuine exception is correctly classified? What is the fail-class recall on silent failures specifically?\n\n2. For the label-only loss ablation: did you control for the effective learning rate on the verdict token? When loss is computed on 1 token vs. ~1000 tokens, the gradient magnitude on the verdict token differs substantially. Did you try adjusting the learning rate to account for this?\n\n3. The DeepSeek seed-123 outlier (91.09%) is attributed to \"LoRA initialization sensitivity,\" but the paper acknowledges \"direct evidence is lacking.\" Have you investigated the specific LoRA weight initialization that caused this? Is it reproducible with the same seed?\n\n4. For the DebugBench evaluation: what is the exception rate among fail-class samples? If DebugBench bugs predominantly raise exceptions (which synthetic mutations often do), then 100% accuracy is expected and does not test the method's limits.\n\n5. The paper claims exception_only > VOTA (p=0.01). But VOTA is a superset of exception_only (it includes exceptions + assertions + loops). Can you explain mechanistically why adding information strictly degrades performance? Is this an attention dilution effect, or is the VOTA formatting introducing confusing tokens?\n\n## 5. Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n1. **Silent logic error analysis.** Stratify fail-class samples by whether an exception was raised. Report exception_only accuracy on the \"no exception raised but test failed\" subset. This is the critical failure mode for the proposed approach.\n\n2. **Encoder-based baseline.** Compare against TRACED or a fine-tuned CodeBERT/CodeT5 on the same task and data. This contextualizes the contribution relative to the most directly relevant prior work.\n\n3. **Simple classifier baseline.** Train a logistic regression or small MLP on binary features (exception present/absent, exception type one-hot) to quantify how much of the signal is capturable without an LLM.\n\n4. **Larger/real-world dataset.** The 591-problem, single-language benchmark is the main limitation. Even a preliminary evaluation on a larger Python dataset (e.g., CodeContests, APPS) would strengthen claims.\n\n5. **Cross-model loop_only and ae_only.** Extend the component ablation to at least one other model to validate the two-tier hierarchy is not Qwen-specific.\n\n6. **Effect of class balance.** The 71.5/28.5 split means the majority baseline is already 71.5%. How does exception_only perform under more balanced or more extreme class ratios?\n\n## 6. Minor Issues\n\n- The dream report paper version uses em-dashes (400--500) throughout, which is fine for number ranges but should be checked for non-range usage per the writing norms.\n- Table 1 in the dream version has missing F1 values (\"---\") for DeepSeek and CodeLlama, reducing comparability.\n- The \"gradient ratio 8.8:1\" metric is non-standard. It would be clearer to simply report the accuracy deltas and let readers assess the steepness.\n- The paper could benefit from a single consolidated results table rather than spreading results across many small tables.\n- Some numbers differ slightly between the dream paper and the actual paper (e.g., CodeLlama exception_only: 99.83% in dream vs. 99.89% in actual). The review is based primarily on the actual paper.\n\n## 7. Score: 4 (Weak Accept)\n\n**Justification:** The paper presents a thorough and well-executed empirical study. The experimental design is strong: multi-model, multi-seed, with appropriate statistical tests, good baselines, and transparent reporting. The core finding that exception events are sufficient for verdict prediction is convincingly demonstrated within the evaluated setting.\n\nHowever, the contribution is limited by several factors: (1) the finding is partially expected given that verdict prediction on these benchmarks is largely reducible to exception detection (as the 93--99% regex baselines confirm); (2) the dataset is small and homogeneous, with limited failure diversity; (3) the \"causal decomposition\" and \"phase transition\" claims are overclaimed relative to their actual content; (4) the OOD and frontier-model comparisons in the dream version appear to be imagined rather than real; and (5) the absence of silent-logic-error analysis leaves the most important practical limitation unaddressed.\n\nThe paper is above the reject threshold because the ablation methodology is exemplary, the multi-model validation is convincing, and the practical implications (use compact exception signals rather than full traces) are useful for practitioners. It falls short of a clear accept because the intellectual contribution, once stripped of framing inflation, is narrower than the paper suggests.\n\nAt a 25% acceptance rate venue, this is borderline. The execution quality argues for acceptance; the limited novelty and overclaiming argue against.\n\n## 8. Confidence: 4/5\n\nI have substantial expertise in code LLM evaluation, SFT methodology, and execution trace analysis. I am confident in my assessment of the experimental design, statistical methodology, and the distinction between real and imagined results. I am slightly less certain about the broader fault localization literature and whether the findings would generalize to production settings.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":685,"cache_read_input_tokens":92667,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":685,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01X88DADNBF2BNK2aeab7Kos","session_id":"dcb29bac-0892-40d8-9a83-9cc5c54535a5","uuid":"9708af93-366b-4fe2-a2cd-937bdbe67ad3"}
